# Inga @VERSION@ -- source for review

This archive contains everything needed to rebuild the submitted
Firefox package from source. The extension is written in Dart and
compiled to JavaScript with `dart compile js` (dart2js), which is why
the submitted package contains generated files.

## What is here

    dart-inga/     the extension, tag @TAG@
    dart-webext/   the shared library it depends on, tag @WEBEXT_TAG@

Both are reduced to what the build reads: sources, manifest, static
files, licences and the build scripts. Tests, documentation, changelogs
and project readmes are not included, as they take no part in producing
the compiled output.

Both projects are public:

    https://github.com/heikozim/dart-inga/tree/@TAG@
    https://github.com/heikozim/dart-webext/tree/@WEBEXT_TAG@

`dart-inga/pubspec.yaml` pins the library to tag `@WEBEXT_TAG@`. The pin
names the private development remote, so
`dart-inga/pubspec_overrides.yaml` redirects the dependency to
`../dart-webext` in this archive, which holds exactly that tagged
state -- the build fetches nothing. The pin itself is left untouched,
so it stays visible to you rather than being quietly rewritten.

`dart-inga/pubspec.lock` is included so dependency resolution matches
the submitted build.

## Build environment

    @SDK@

Install from https://dart.dev/get-dart. No Node.js, no npm, no bundler,
no minifier and no network access beyond `dart pub get`.

## Build

    cd dart-inga
    dart pub get
    tool/build_firefox.sh

The result is written to `dart-inga/build-firefox/ext/`, which is the
directory that was zipped and submitted.

The script does three things and nothing else:

1. `dart compile js -Dfirefox=true` once per entry point in `web/`.
2. Removes the `.map` and `.deps` files dart2js writes beside each
   output, and strips the `sourceMappingURL` line from the three `.js`
   files. Source maps are not shipped, so the bare reference would only
   make the developer tools report 404s.
3. Copies the static files word for word and generates the Firefox
   manifest from `manifest.json` via `tool/firefox_manifest.dart`
   (this adds `browser_specific_settings.gecko` and turns the
   background service worker into an event page script).

## No minification or obfuscation

dart2js is the only transformation applied. The output is machine
generated and therefore not laid out like handwritten JavaScript, but
no minifier, packer, obfuscator or name mangler runs on top of it, and
nothing is fetched or generated at install or run time.

## Verify

    cd build-firefox/ext
    shasum -a 256 build/*.js manifest.json

Expected, and identical to the submitted package:

@HASHES@

These sums were taken from the very build that was packaged for
submission. Reproducibility across other operating systems or Dart
versions was not tested, so a different SDK may legitimately differ.

## Why the extension asks for all hosts

Inga inspects the HTTP headers of whatever page the user is looking at.
Which sites those are is not known in advance, so the host permission
cannot be narrowed to a list. The permission is used only to observe
requests that the browser makes anyway, through `webRequest`; the
extension never modifies, blocks or redirects a request, and it injects
nothing into pages except `inject/protocol.js`, a five-line script that
reads `performance.getEntriesByType("navigation")[0].nextHopProtocol`
and returns it.

The extension performs no outgoing request on its own. The single
request it can make is the `/cdn-cgi/trace` fetch in `web/popup.dart`:
off by default, enabled only in the options page, and then fired only
by a click on the word `cloudflare`. It goes to the origin of the
inspected page, without path or query. Captured headers stay in
`storage.session` and are never sent anywhere. This is why the manifest
declares `data_collection_permissions` as `none`.

